ial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482509 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is defined as the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition, ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},$ is exactly line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing }{Φ}_{0}(0)\\text{ with }{I}_{n}\\text{ in }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482582 #edb/ref 1074 "% Explanation\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482598 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482598 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482614 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L6}$, and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L6}$, we substitute $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can combine the exponentials:\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7}$ gives line $\\text{L8}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{the fact that }{e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482640 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482640 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482645 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482645 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482645 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1447d08-16ad-539a-8ebf-f17fc52a738b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229684 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
